// src/user/a11y/kind_a11y_node.pdx -- Wave0-B14 G10-M1-002 (paideia-os #2303)
//
// KIND_A11Y_NODE = 0x1E2, LINEAR cap derived over KIND_A11Y_TREE (0x1D3).
// Design authority: design/compositor/pwp-spec-vocabulary.md §2.22
// (KIND_A11Y_NODE -- "one node in an accessibility tree -- role, state,
// value, name, description, and edges to siblings and children"). The
// spec's §2.22 prose marks linearity as "non-linear"; the row prompt
// for this landing (G10-M1-002) pins it LINEAR instead -- one mint per
// subtree attach, consumed on subtree_detach -- and that is the
// authoritative shape this file implements. See DEVIATION FROM SPEC
// §2.22 below for why the two disagree and which one wins.
//
// SLUG CORRECTION. The row prompt driving this landing labelled itself
// "G10-M1-001"; the live GitHub issue (#2303, title "KIND_A11Y_NODE
// catalogue: role/state/value node schemas") states its own slug is
// G10-M1-002 and that it DEPENDS ON G10-M1-001 (#2302, KIND_A11Y_TREE,
// already landed as src/user/a11y/tree_kind.pdx, Wave0-B8-03). The
// verify-fingerprint-coverage.sh allowlist already carries two entries
// tagged "G10-M1-001 (#2302)" for that prior landing -- reusing the
// same slug for #2303 would collide two different milestones under one
// label in that ledger. This file cites the self-consistent slug
// (G10-M1-002, #2303) throughout; the batch label (Wave0-B14) is kept
// as given.
//
// FILE-PATH NOTE. Issue #2303's own "Files touched" field names
// `src/user/a11y/node_kind.pdx` (matching the directory's own
// `<noun>_kind.pdx` convention -- see tree_kind.pdx). This landing's
// row prompt instead named this file explicitly as
// `kind_a11y_node.pdx` (the `kind_<name>.pdx` convention used under
// src/kernel/core/cap/). The row prompt's explicit, deliberate naming
// is honoured here; flagged for main's attention in case a follow-on
// wants the two renamed into alignment.
//
// DEVIATION FROM SPEC §2.22 (linearity). §2.22 lists KIND_A11Y_NODE as
// "non-linear", matching how tree_kind.pdx already stores nodes
// in-line inside each KIND_A11Y_TREE arena (a fixed 32-node array per
// tree, packed 32 bytes/node, no independent per-node capability). This
// file's row prompt asks for a DIFFERENT shape: KIND_A11Y_NODE as its
// own LINEAR cap-table citizen, one row per live node, minted at
// subtree-attach time and consumed at subtree-detach time, addressed by
// a kernel-assigned u64 node_id rather than an arena array index. The
// two designs are not in conflict at the data-model level -- a future
// landing can have the compositor drive tree_kind.pdx's arena as the
// FAST PATH read/walk structure while KIND_A11Y_NODE is the CAPABILITY
// that names one node for cross-process hand-off (e.g. "focus THIS
// node" from an input-route dispatch, or "describe THIS node" to an
// out-of-process screen reader) -- but that reconciliation is a
// follow-on design note, not something this schema-only landing
// resolves. This file follows the row prompt's LINEAR framing because
// that is the explicit, current instruction; the spec's "non-linear"
// line should be revisited (and probably reworded to describe
// tree_kind.pdx's arena nodes specifically) the next time
// pwp-spec-vocabulary.md gets a freeze-surface edit.
//
// LANDING SCOPE (schema + stub, mirrors src/user/ime/session_kind.pdx
// one-for-one in shape -- see that file's own header for the precedent
// this one follows almost verbatim): ordinal, parent, rights, row
// layout (identity fields + role/state/value schema), op catalog,
// failure taxonomy, fingerprint, packers/accessors/validators (pure
// leaves), and the three op functions named by the row prompt --
// a11y_node_mint, a11y_node_attach_child, a11y_node_detach -- as STUB
// BODIES. Every stub runs its real argument-shape gates (the pure
// validators declared here) and returns NODE_NOT_INIT on any gate that
// passes, because the row-pool substrate that would actually allocate,
// link and free a row DOES NOT EXIST YET in this landing. A stub that
// fabricated a row id or a success return would defeat the LINEAR
// invariant this file exists to establish -- exactly the discipline
// session_kind.pdx's own stub-body header calls out for KIND_IME_SESSION.
// The future substrate landing owns: `_a11y_node_rows` pool storage,
// tail_alloc / tail_free, cap_table wiring, cap_handler_a11y_node
// dispatch, and the klog_s1_x1 emission of this file's fingerprint on
// a real successful mint.
//
// =============================================================================
// SECTION 0 -- KIND SLOT ALLOCATION
// =============================================================================
//
// The row prompt's suggested band (0x1B8..0x1DE) is grep-verified FULL
// at landing time -- every ordinal in that inclusive range already has
// a live `pub let ... : u64 = 0x1XX` declaration somewhere under src/
// (surface/window/layer-tree/subsurface/clipboard/dnd/selection/
// recovery-plane/workspace/a11y-tree/ime-session/ui-context/damage/
// buffer-age/seat/input-route/touch-contact/stylus/ime-provider/
// ime-router families, per a repo-wide `grep -rn "pub let.*: u64 =
// 0x1[BCD][0-9A-Fa-f]\b"` sweep). 0x1DF is likewise claimed
// (ime_router.pdx's own reserved-successor note). The row prompt
// authorizes falling through to 0x1E0..0x1FF when the primary band is
// full; that fallback band has exactly two live claims --
// KIND_TTS_ENGINE (0x1E0, screen_reader_tts.pdx, forward-reserved) and
// KIND_BRAILLE_DEVICE (0x1E1, screen_reader_braille.pdx, forward-
// reserved) -- both LOGIC-MODULE forward reservations, not landed
// KIND-cap files, but their ordinals are live numeric claims either
// way. 0x1E2 is the next free slot and is what this file takes.
// (0x1ED and 0x1F0 also appear in a raw grep of that range but resolve
// to unrelated numeric literals -- a directory mode 0755 in
// rootfs_seed.pdx and a page-table index mask in vtd_slpt.pdx / the
// generic 0x1FF octal-mode literal -- neither is a KIND ordinal
// declaration, so neither collides.)
//
//   KIND_A11Y_NODE : u64 = 0x1E2
//
// Main should advance the "Next free derived-kind tag" bookkeeping in
// design/architecture/next-wave-derived-kinds.md past 0x1E2 at batch
// close, and should reconcile pwp-spec-vocabulary.md's own derivation
// graph (§3) once the 0x1B8..0x1DF band-exhaustion note above is
// folded back into that document.
//
// =============================================================================
// SECTION 1 -- DERIVATION OVER KIND_A11Y_TREE
// =============================================================================
//
// Per spec §2.22, the parent kind is KIND_A11Y_TREE (0x1D3, landed at
// src/user/a11y/tree_kind.pdx, Wave0-B8-03, paideia-os #2302). This
// file references that ordinal by numeric literal (A11Y_NODE_PARENT_
// KIND) rather than importing TreeKind's module, sidestepping a
// module-order dependency in the encoder's linker step -- the exact
// convention session_kind.pdx documents for its own KIND_WINDOW
// reference. The coordination point remains the derived-kinds registry,
// which lists every ordinal atomically at batch close.
//
// A future mint gate (landed alongside the real row-pool substrate)
// checks: parent tree_ref names a live KIND_A11Y_TREE row. This file's
// a11y_node_mint stub checks only that tree_ref is nonzero (the cheap,
// pool-independent half of that gate); the "is this actually a live
// row" half needs a call into tree_kind.pdx's a11y_tree_row_valid and
// is deferred to the substrate landing alongside the pool itself, to
// keep this file's touch-set at ONE file per the row prompt's scope.
//
// =============================================================================
// SECTION 2 -- LINEARITY DISCIPLINE
// =============================================================================
//
//   * NO R_MINT bit is declared on this kind (mirrors KIND_IME_SESSION
//     exactly). A KIND_A11Y_NODE row is minted BY the a11y subtree-
//     attach authority (the compositor's a11y substrate, once landed)
//     and never by an arbitrary client holding the parent tree cap.
//   * Consume-on-detach contract: a11y_node_detach is the LINEAR
//     consume. One detach per mint. A second detach against an already-
//     detached (or never-minted) node_id is refused, not silently
//     accepted -- avoiding IME_SESSION's exact "ALREADY_REVOKED" shape
//     is out of scope here only because there is no live row state yet
//     to double-check against; the real substrate MUST add that check
//     when it lands (see NODE_NOT_INIT's own note below).
//   * a11y_node_attach_child does NOT consume either argument -- it
//     links an already-minted child node under an already-minted
//     parent node by node_id reference. Only a11y_node_detach consumes.
//   * UPDATE_STATE (a future op; declared in the op catalog below,
//     stub body deferred to the substrate landing since it needs live
//     row storage to mutate) mutates the state bitmask IN PLACE; it
//     does not consume the cap.
//
// =============================================================================
// SECTION 3 -- FAILURE TAXONOMY  0xFFFFE840..0xFFFFE85F
// =============================================================================
//
// Pre-assigned by the row prompt; grep-verified free across src/ at
// landing time (`grep -rn "0xFFFFE84[0-9A-Fa-f]\|0xFFFFE85[0-9A-Fa-f]"
// src/**/*.pdx` returns nothing). Nearest occupied neighbours: bidi_uba.
// pdx's 0xFFFFE800..80F (G11.M4 IME family) sits 0x40 below this band's
// floor; nothing else claims 0xFFFFE810..E8FF as of this landing. The
// eight codes named by the row prompt are assigned from the TOP of the
// 32-slot band downward (0x5F first), leaving 0xFFFFE840..E857 (24
// slots) free for the substrate landing's own additions (e.g. a real
// ALREADY_DETACHED / DOUBLE_DETACH code, mirroring IME_SESSION's
// ALREADY_REVOKED, once live row state exists to detect the double
// consume against).
//
// =============================================================================
// SECTION 4 -- FINGERPRINT
// =============================================================================
//
// One legacy-suffixed tag, per the standard klog convention (matches
// tree_kind.pdx / session_kind.pdx / kind_tui_canvas.pdx precedent).
// Data-only at this landing -- no code path emits it yet, because
// a11y_node_mint's success arm cannot be reached until the row-pool
// substrate lands (see LANDING SCOPE above). Needs allowlisting in
// tools/verify-fingerprint-coverage.sh with reason "kind_a11y_node meta
// emit body not yet landed (Wave0-B14 G10-M1-002, #2303)" until the
// substrate lands and wires klog_s1_x1 into a real mint success tail.
// That allowlist entry is added by this same landing per the row
// prompt's own instruction.
//
// LABEL PREFIX: `a11y_node_` throughout -- sidesteps paideia-as
// reserved-keyword collisions (`loop`, `if`, `for`, `match`, `let`, ...)
// per feedback_paideia_as_reserved_labels.
//
// =============================================================================

module KindA11yNode = structure {

  // ==========================================================================
  // SECTION A.1 -- KIND ORDINAL + PARENT
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let KIND_A11Y_NODE        : u64 = 0x1E2
  pub let A11Y_NODE_PARENT_KIND : u64 = 0x1D3   // = KIND_A11Y_TREE (tree_kind.pdx, #2302)

  // ==========================================================================
  // SECTION A.2 -- RIGHTS
  // ==========================================================================
  //
  // Sparse bit map (surface_commit / kind_tui_canvas / tonemap_kind
  // style) so a future dispatcher can `and rax, R_*` and branch on one
  // bit. CRITICALLY: there is no R_A11Y_NODE_MINT bit -- see SECTION 2
  // above. ATTACH_CHILD is listed as compositor-internal (the substrate
  // that owns subtree structure), not a client-invocable right, mirroring
  // tree_kind.pdx's R_A11Y_APPEND_NODE / R_A11Y_UPDATE_NODE treatment.
  //
  //   R_A11Y_NODE_READ          -- query role / state / value fields
  //   R_A11Y_NODE_UPDATE_STATE  -- mutate the state bitmask in place
  //                                (does not consume the cap)
  //   R_A11Y_NODE_ATTACH_CHILD  -- compositor-internal: link a child
  //                                node_id under this node
  //   R_A11Y_NODE_DETACH        -- LINEAR consume: detach + free
  //   R_A11Y_NODE_OBSERVE       -- compositor-held debug/subscribe path

  pub let R_A11Y_NODE_READ         : u64 = 0x01
  pub let R_A11Y_NODE_UPDATE_STATE : u64 = 0x02
  pub let R_A11Y_NODE_ATTACH_CHILD : u64 = 0x04   // compositor-internal
  pub let R_A11Y_NODE_DETACH       : u64 = 0x08
  pub let R_A11Y_NODE_OBSERVE      : u64 = 0x10
  pub let R_A11Y_NODE_ALL          : u64 = 0x1F

  // Client-visible right-set at mint time: everything except OBSERVE
  // (compositor-only debug path) and ATTACH_CHILD (compositor-internal
  // structural op) -- mirrors R_IME_SESSION_CLIENT's carve-out shape.
  pub let R_A11Y_NODE_CLIENT       : u64 = 0x0B   // READ | UPDATE_STATE | DETACH

  // ==========================================================================
  // SECTION B.1 -- ROLE ENUM  (A11yNodeRole, u16, ~40 canonical roles)
  // ==========================================================================
  //
  // ARIA-superset subset chosen for Wave 0 breadth: every role the row
  // prompt named explicitly (button, link, textbox, list, listitem,
  // heading, region) plus the widget/composite/landmark roles most
  // toolkits emit in practice (menus, tabs, trees, grids, sliders,
  // dialogs). ROLE_MAX is the last legal ordinal (inclusive); a
  // validator uses `cmp role, ROLE_MAX; ja <reject>` so extending the
  // enum is a one-line ROLE_MAX bump that stays inside the u16 field.
  // Ordinals are stable once landed -- a role's numeric value is part
  // of the wire contract the moment any client observes it.

  pub let ROLE_UNKNOWN           : u64 = 0
  pub let ROLE_WINDOW            : u64 = 1
  pub let ROLE_BUTTON            : u64 = 2
  pub let ROLE_LINK              : u64 = 3
  pub let ROLE_TEXTBOX           : u64 = 4
  pub let ROLE_LABEL             : u64 = 5
  pub let ROLE_CHECKBOX          : u64 = 6
  pub let ROLE_RADIO             : u64 = 7
  pub let ROLE_RADIOGROUP        : u64 = 8
  pub let ROLE_COMBOBOX          : u64 = 9
  pub let ROLE_LISTBOX           : u64 = 10
  pub let ROLE_LIST              : u64 = 11
  pub let ROLE_LISTITEM          : u64 = 12
  pub let ROLE_MENU              : u64 = 13
  pub let ROLE_MENUBAR           : u64 = 14
  pub let ROLE_MENUITEM          : u64 = 15
  pub let ROLE_TAB               : u64 = 16
  pub let ROLE_TABLIST           : u64 = 17
  pub let ROLE_TABPANEL          : u64 = 18
  pub let ROLE_TREE              : u64 = 19
  pub let ROLE_TREEITEM          : u64 = 20
  pub let ROLE_GRID              : u64 = 21
  pub let ROLE_GRIDCELL          : u64 = 22
  pub let ROLE_ROW               : u64 = 23
  pub let ROLE_ROWHEADER         : u64 = 24
  pub let ROLE_COLUMNHEADER      : u64 = 25
  pub let ROLE_TABLE             : u64 = 26
  pub let ROLE_CELL              : u64 = 27
  pub let ROLE_SLIDER            : u64 = 28
  pub let ROLE_SPINBUTTON        : u64 = 29
  pub let ROLE_PROGRESSBAR       : u64 = 30
  pub let ROLE_SCROLLBAR         : u64 = 31
  pub let ROLE_SWITCH            : u64 = 32
  pub let ROLE_TOOLBAR           : u64 = 33
  pub let ROLE_TOOLTIP           : u64 = 34
  pub let ROLE_DIALOG            : u64 = 35
  pub let ROLE_ALERT             : u64 = 36
  pub let ROLE_HEADING           : u64 = 37
  pub let ROLE_REGION            : u64 = 38
  pub let ROLE_IMAGE             : u64 = 39
  pub let ROLE_MAX               : u64 = 39   // 40 legal ordinals: 0..39

  // ==========================================================================
  // SECTION B.2 -- STATE BITMASK  (A11yNodeState, u32)
  // ==========================================================================
  //
  // The seven channels the row prompt named explicitly (focused,
  // pressed, expanded, checked, disabled, hidden, busy) occupy bits
  // 0..6; three more common AccessKit/ARIA channels (selected, readonly,
  // required) occupy 7..9 so the field earns its u32 width instead of
  // being a disguised u8 like tree_kind.pdx's 8-bit STATE_MASK_ALL.
  // Bits 10..31 are reserved -- a caller that sets them is refused by
  // a11y_node_state_valid (STATE_MASK_ALL below), so a future widening
  // never has to reason about stale bit patterns left by an earlier
  // client.

  pub let STATE_FOCUSED    : u64 = 0x001
  pub let STATE_PRESSED    : u64 = 0x002
  pub let STATE_EXPANDED   : u64 = 0x004
  pub let STATE_CHECKED    : u64 = 0x008
  pub let STATE_DISABLED   : u64 = 0x010
  pub let STATE_HIDDEN     : u64 = 0x020
  pub let STATE_BUSY       : u64 = 0x040
  pub let STATE_SELECTED   : u64 = 0x080
  pub let STATE_READONLY   : u64 = 0x100
  pub let STATE_REQUIRED   : u64 = 0x200
  pub let STATE_MASK_ALL   : u64 = 0x3FF   // OR of every bit above

  // ==========================================================================
  // SECTION B.3 -- ROW LAYOUT  (A11yNodeRow, 128 bytes = 2 cache lines)
  // ==========================================================================
  //
  // One row per live KIND_A11Y_NODE cap (future substrate storage; no
  // pool array is declared in THIS landing -- see LANDING SCOPE). The
  // row is split into an IDENTITY half (byte 0..63, one cache line:
  // header, node_id, edges by node_id reference, owning tree, packed
  // role/state) and a VALUE half (byte 64..127, one cache line: the
  // seven-field AccessKit-shape value struct the row prompt specifies).
  // Splitting on the cache-line boundary means a pure structural walk
  // (role + edges, e.g. a screen reader's tree traversal) never has to
  // pull in the value half's cache line, and a pure value read (e.g.
  // "what does this slider currently say") never has to pull in the
  // identity half beyond the one word it already holds a reference to.
  //
  //   [+0]   header:          in_use[63:56] | reserved[55:0]
  //   [+8]   node_id:         u64 -- kernel-assigned monotonic identity;
  //                                  0 = NODE_ID_NONE (never a live id)
  //   [+16]  parent_node_id:  u64 -- NODE_ID_NONE at the subtree root
  //   [+24]  first_child_id:  u64 -- NODE_ID_NONE if leaf
  //   [+32]  next_sibling_id: u64 -- NODE_ID_NONE terminates the chain
  //   [+40]  tree_ref:        u64 -- owning KIND_A11Y_TREE row/cap-table
  //                                  slot (A11Y_NODE_PARENT_KIND's
  //                                  concrete row this node lives under)
  //   [+48]  role_state:      role[15:0] | state[47:16] | reserved[63:48]
  //   [+56]  reserved0:       u64 -- pads the identity half to 64 bytes
  //   -------------------------------------------------------------------
  //   [+64]  value.name_ptr:       u64 -- interned string / memory
  //                                       offset naming the node (a
  //                                       button's label, a heading's
  //                                       text run)
  //   [+72]  value.desc_ptr:       u64 -- interned string offset for the
  //                                       long-form accessible
  //                                       description (0 = none)
  //   [+80]  value.value_text_ptr: u64 -- interned string offset for a
  //                                       textual value (combobox
  //                                       reading, textbox contents;
  //                                       0 = none)
  //   [+88]  value.value_num:      i64 -- signed numeric value (slider
  //                                       position, spinbutton reading)
  //   [+96]  value.min:            i64 -- signed lower bound for value_num
  //   [+104] value.max:            i64 -- signed upper bound for value_num
  //   [+112] value.step:           i64 -- signed step increment for
  //                                       value_num
  //   [+120] reserved1:            u64 -- pads the value half to 64 bytes
  //
  // Fields at [+88..+112] are documented as signed (i64) because a
  // value_num / min / max / step is meaningful below zero (e.g. a pan
  // slider centred at 0, or a temperature spinbutton with a negative
  // range); the storage cell itself is a raw 64-bit word manipulated
  // with plain `mov` (no arithmetic on these fields is defined in this
  // landing, so no signed-vs-unsigned opcode choice arises here yet --
  // the future UPDATE_VALUE op is what will need to pick signed compare/
  // shift forms when it lands).

  pub let NODE_ROW_BYTES  : u64 = 128
  pub let A11Y_NODE_MAX   : u64 = 64    // Wave 0 pool ceiling for the
                                        // future substrate; 64 * 128 =
                                        // 8192 bytes = 1024 u64 words.
                                        // A future landing may grow this
                                        // without a row-shape break.

  // Identity-half offsets.
  pub let NODE_OFF_HEADER           : u64 = 0
  pub let NODE_OFF_NODE_ID          : u64 = 8
  pub let NODE_OFF_PARENT_ID        : u64 = 16
  pub let NODE_OFF_FIRST_CHILD_ID   : u64 = 24
  pub let NODE_OFF_NEXT_SIBLING_ID  : u64 = 32
  pub let NODE_OFF_TREE_REF         : u64 = 40
  pub let NODE_OFF_ROLE_STATE       : u64 = 48
  pub let NODE_OFF_RESERVED0        : u64 = 56

  // Value-half offsets (the row prompt's seven-field value struct).
  pub let NODE_OFF_VALUE_NAME_PTR       : u64 = 64
  pub let NODE_OFF_VALUE_DESC_PTR       : u64 = 72
  pub let NODE_OFF_VALUE_TEXT_PTR       : u64 = 80
  pub let NODE_OFF_VALUE_NUM            : u64 = 88
  pub let NODE_OFF_VALUE_MIN            : u64 = 96
  pub let NODE_OFF_VALUE_MAX            : u64 = 104
  pub let NODE_OFF_VALUE_STEP           : u64 = 112
  pub let NODE_OFF_RESERVED1            : u64 = 120

  // Header bit position (top byte of the [+0] word). No `revoked` bit
  // is declared yet -- the future substrate lands it alongside the
  // real LINEAR double-detach guard (see SECTION 2 + NODE_NOT_INIT's
  // own note).
  pub let NODE_HDR_IN_USE_BIT : u64 = 56

  // Reference sentinel: 0 means "no such node" for parent_node_id /
  // first_child_id / next_sibling_id / node_id / tree_ref alike. A
  // fresh mint's kernel-assigned node_id is pre-incremented so the
  // first live id is 1, guaranteeing 0 stays free as the sentinel
  // (mirrors kind_tui_canvas.pdx's tui_next_canvas_id / canvas_id
  // convention one-for-one).
  pub let NODE_ID_NONE : u64 = 0

  // ==========================================================================
  // SECTION B.4 -- OP CATALOG
  // ==========================================================================
  //
  // Every QUERY_* op is arity ONE (node_id in target_ptr, field
  // selector in op_arg[7:0]; op_arg[63:8] MUST be zero). ATTACH_CHILD
  // and DETACH are NOT arity-one ops -- ATTACH_CHILD takes two node_id
  // arguments and DETACH is the LINEAR consume; both land as
  // compositor-internal helpers (a11y_node_attach_child /
  // a11y_node_detach below) exactly as tree_kind.pdx's APPEND_NODE /
  // UPDATE_STATE / SUBSCRIBE are NOT arity-one ops either. Real
  // dispatch (cap_handler_a11y_node) is a future kernel-side milestone
  // that follows the row-pool substrate landing.

  pub let NODE_OP_QUERY_ROLE    : u64 = 0
  pub let NODE_OP_QUERY_STATE   : u64 = 1
  pub let NODE_OP_QUERY_PARENT  : u64 = 2
  pub let NODE_OP_QUERY_VALUE   : u64 = 3   // sub-selector: see FIELD_* below
  pub let NODE_OP_UPDATE_STATE  : u64 = 4
  pub let NODE_OP_DEBUG_PRINT   : u64 = 5
  pub let NODE_OP_MAX           : u64 = 5

  // QUERY_VALUE field selectors (payload sub-op, mirrors IME_SESSION's
  // QUERY_STATE field-id shape).
  pub let NODE_FIELD_NAME       : u64 = 0
  pub let NODE_FIELD_DESC       : u64 = 1
  pub let NODE_FIELD_VALUE_TEXT : u64 = 2
  pub let NODE_FIELD_VALUE_NUM  : u64 = 3
  pub let NODE_FIELD_MIN        : u64 = 4
  pub let NODE_FIELD_MAX        : u64 = 5
  pub let NODE_FIELD_STEP       : u64 = 6
  pub let NODE_FIELD_MAX_ID     : u64 = 6

  // ==========================================================================
  // SECTION B.5 -- FAILURE TAXONOMY  0xFFFFE840..0xFFFFE85F
  // ==========================================================================
  //
  // See file header SECTION 3 for the band-allocation rationale. The
  // eight codes below are the row prompt's exact reserve list, assigned
  // top-down from the 32-slot band's ceiling (0x5F); 0xFFFFE840..E857
  // (24 slots) stays free for the substrate landing.

  pub let A11Y_NODE_OK              : u64 = 0
  pub let NODE_REFUSED_DENSE        : u64 = 0xFFFFE85F   // pool full (future ENOSPC)
  pub let NODE_VALUE_TYPE_MISMATCH  : u64 = 0xFFFFE85E   // future: role's expected
                                                          // value shape (numeric vs
                                                          // text) doesn't match the
                                                          // fields supplied
  pub let NODE_CYCLE                : u64 = 0xFFFFE85D   // attach would create a cycle
  pub let NODE_PARENT_MISSING       : u64 = 0xFFFFE85C   // tree_ref / parent node absent
  pub let NODE_STATE_CONFLICT       : u64 = 0xFFFFE85B   // state has reserved bits set
  pub let NODE_ROLE_INVALID         : u64 = 0xFFFFE85A   // role > ROLE_MAX
  pub let NODE_NULL                 : u64 = 0xFFFFE859   // a required node_id arg is 0
  pub let NODE_NOT_INIT             : u64 = 0xFFFFE858   // row-pool substrate not yet
                                                          // landed; every gate that
                                                          // would otherwise succeed
                                                          // funnels here in THIS
                                                          // landing (see LANDING SCOPE)

  // ==========================================================================
  // SECTION B.6 -- FINGERPRINT
  // ==========================================================================
  //
  // Emitted once by the future substrate on a real successful mint; not
  // emitted by any code path in this landing (see file header SECTION
  // 4). "a11y node init ok [legacy: A11Y NODE INIT OK]\0" = 45 bytes of
  // payload + 1 NUL terminator = 46 bytes total.
  pub let pdx_kind_a11y_node_meta : [u8; 46] = "a11y node init ok [legacy: A11Y NODE INIT OK]\0"

  // ==========================================================================
  // SECTION C -- PACKERS + ACCESSORS  (pure leaves, no branches)
  // ==========================================================================

  // a11y_node_hdr_pack() -> canonical header at mint time. Only in_use
  // (bit 56) is ever written, and only by the future substrate's
  // tail_alloc; this file's stub never allocates a row so it never
  // calls this itself, but the packer is landed now for the substrate
  // to reuse verbatim (mirrors tui_hdr_pack / ime_session_hdr_pack /
  // a11y_tree_pack_hdr_row precedent).
  pub let a11y_node_hdr_pack : () -> u64 !{} @{} =
    fn () -> unsafe {
      effects: {}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "Wave0-B14 G10-M1-002 (paideia-os #2303): canonical row header. Only in_use (bit 56) is ever written, by the future substrate's tail_alloc. Straight-line, no labels needed.",
      block: {
        xor rax, rax;
        ret
      }
    }

  // a11y_node_pack_role_state(role, state) -> role[15:0] | state[47:16].
  // role masked to 16 bits, state masked to 32 bits then shifted into
  // place; reserved bits 48..63 stay zero. `and rax, imm32` / `and r10,
  // imm32` are both encoder-legal (only `and r11, imm64` is not).
  pub let a11y_node_pack_role_state : (u64, u64) -> u64 !{} @{} =
    fn (role: u64) (state: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "Wave0-B14 G10-M1-002 (paideia-os #2303): pack role_state word at row +48. role masked to 16 bits (0xFFFF); state masked to 32 bits (0xFFFFFFFF) then shifted left 16 into place. Reserved bits 48..63 remain zero. Straight-line, no labels needed.",
      block: {
        mov rax, rdi;
        and rax, 0xFFFF;
        mov r10, rsi;
        and r10, 0xFFFFFFFF;
        shl r10, 16;
        or  rax, r10;
        ret
      }
    }

  // a11y_node_role_of(role_state_word) -> role (low 16 bits).
  pub let a11y_node_role_of : (u64) -> u64 !{} @{} =
    fn (role_state_word: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "Wave0-B14 G10-M1-002 (paideia-os #2303): extract role from a packed role_state word. Mask low 16 bits. Straight-line.",
      block: {
        mov rax, rdi;
        and rax, 0xFFFF;
        ret
      }
    }

  // a11y_node_state_of(role_state_word) -> state (bits [47:16]).
  pub let a11y_node_state_of : (u64) -> u64 !{} @{} =
    fn (role_state_word: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "Wave0-B14 G10-M1-002 (paideia-os #2303): extract state from a packed role_state word. shr 16 then mask 32 bits. Straight-line.",
      block: {
        mov rax, rdi;
        shr rax, 16;
        and rax, 0xFFFFFFFF;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // SECTION D -- VALIDATORS  (pure leaves, one branch each)
  // ==========================================================================

  // a11y_node_role_valid(role) -> 1 iff role <= ROLE_MAX(39). Reused by
  // a11y_node_mint today and by the future UPDATE-role path (if one
  // ever lands -- role is documented as immutable-after-mint in every
  // AccessKit-lineage implementation surveyed, so no ROLE-mutating op
  // is declared in SECTION B.4 above).
  pub let a11y_node_role_valid : (u64) -> u64 !{} @{} =
    fn (role: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "Wave0-B14 G10-M1-002 (paideia-os #2303): 1 iff role in [0, ROLE_MAX(39)]. Rejects garbage values that would corrupt the packed role_state word's low 16 bits with a value no dispatcher recognises. Labels prefixed a11y_node_rov_.",
      block: {
        mov r10, 39;
        cmp rdi, r10;
        ja  a11y_node_rov_no;
        mov rax, 1;
        ret;
      a11y_node_rov_no:
        xor rax, rax;
        ret
      }
    }

  // a11y_node_state_valid(state) -> 1 iff state subset of
  // STATE_MASK_ALL(0x3FF). Reused by a11y_node_mint and by the future
  // a11y_node_update_state op.
  pub let a11y_node_state_valid : (u64) -> u64 !{} @{} =
    fn (state: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "Wave0-B14 G10-M1-002 (paideia-os #2303): 1 iff state subset of STATE_MASK_ALL(0x3FF). Reserved bits 10..31 are refused so a future widening never has to reason about stale bit patterns left by an earlier client. Labels prefixed a11y_node_sv_.",
      block: {
        mov rax, rdi;
        mov r10, 0x3FF;
        and rax, r10;
        cmp rax, rdi;
        jne a11y_node_sv_no;
        mov rax, 1;
        ret;
      a11y_node_sv_no:
        xor rax, rax;
        ret
      }
    }

  // a11y_node_tree_ref_valid(tree_ref) -> 1 iff tree_ref != NODE_ID_NONE
  // (0). Cheap, pool-independent half of the real parent-gate (see file
  // header SECTION 1); the "is this actually a live KIND_A11Y_TREE row"
  // half needs tree_kind.pdx's a11y_tree_row_valid and is deferred to
  // the substrate landing.
  pub let a11y_node_tree_ref_valid : (u64) -> u64 !{} @{} =
    fn (tree_ref: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "Wave0-B14 G10-M1-002 (paideia-os #2303): 1 iff tree_ref != 0. A free-floating node (no owning tree) is refused with NODE_PARENT_MISSING at the mint gate. Labels prefixed a11y_node_trv_.",
      block: {
        cmp rdi, 0;
        je  a11y_node_trv_no;
        mov rax, 1;
        ret;
      a11y_node_trv_no:
        xor rax, rax;
        ret
      }
    }

  // a11y_node_id_valid(id) -> 1 iff id != NODE_ID_NONE (0). Declared for
  // reuse by the future QUERY_* / UPDATE_STATE op dispatch (every one of
  // those ops takes a node_id target and must reject the sentinel
  // before touching row storage). a11y_node_attach_child and
  // a11y_node_detach below inline this exact check directly instead of
  // calling it, keeping those two functions pure leaves with no nested
  // call to align a stack frame for -- the same "inline rather than
  // call for a one-instruction check" discipline kind_tui_canvas.pdx's
  // tui_canvas_row_valid documents for itself.
  pub let a11y_node_id_valid : (u64) -> u64 !{} @{} =
    fn (id: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "Wave0-B14 G10-M1-002 (paideia-os #2303): 1 iff id != NODE_ID_NONE(0). Declared for reuse by future QUERY_*/UPDATE_STATE dispatch; a11y_node_attach_child/a11y_node_detach inline the equivalent one-instruction check directly to stay pure leaves (no nested call to align for). Labels prefixed a11y_node_idv_.",
      block: {
        cmp rdi, 0;
        je  a11y_node_idv_no;
        mov rax, 1;
        ret;
      a11y_node_idv_no:
        xor rax, rax;
        ret
      }
    }

  // a11y_node_no_self_cycle(parent_id, child_id) -> 1 iff parent_id !=
  // child_id. This is the O(1) trivial-cycle guard only -- a node
  // cannot be attached as its own child. It does NOT walk the full
  // ancestor chain (a deeper cycle, e.g. attaching an ancestor three
  // levels up as a child, needs a live parent-chain walk against real
  // row storage) -- that fuller check is deferred to the substrate
  // landing alongside NODE_CYCLE's real detection path. Declared for
  // reuse by that future landing; a11y_node_attach_child below inlines
  // the equivalent one-instruction check directly (same pure-leaf
  // rationale as a11y_node_id_valid above).
  pub let a11y_node_no_self_cycle : (u64, u64) -> u64 !{} @{} =
    fn (parent_id: u64) (child_id: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "Wave0-B14 G10-M1-002 (paideia-os #2303): 1 iff parent_id != child_id. O(1) trivial self-cycle guard only; a full ancestor-chain walk needs live row storage and is deferred to the substrate landing. Declared for reuse there; a11y_node_attach_child inlines the equivalent check to stay a pure leaf. Labels prefixed a11y_node_nsc_.",
      block: {
        cmp rdi, rsi;
        je  a11y_node_nsc_no;
        mov rax, 1;
        ret;
      a11y_node_nsc_no:
        xor rax, rax;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // SECTION E -- MINT / ATTACH_CHILD / DETACH STUBS
  //
  // Schema-only stubs, mirroring src/user/ime/session_kind.pdx SECTION
  // 3 one-for-one: every function here runs its REAL argument-shape
  // gates against the pure validators in SECTION D, and returns
  // NODE_NOT_INIT on any gate that passes -- because the row-pool
  // substrate that would actually allocate, link, mutate or free a row
  // DOES NOT EXIST in this landing (no `_a11y_node_rows` state cell is
  // declared anywhere in this file). Fabricating a row id or a success
  // return here would defeat the LINEAR invariant this file exists to
  // establish; a caller that gets NODE_NOT_INIT back knows unambiguously
  // that no row was allocated, linked, or freed, rather than being lied
  // to by a stub that pretended the mint succeeded.
  //
  // The future substrate landing owns:
  //   * `_a11y_node_rows` pool storage (A11Y_NODE_MAX * NODE_ROW_BYTES)
  //   * a kernel-assigned monotonic node_id counter (pre-incremented,
  //     mirrors kind_tui_canvas.pdx's tui_next_canvas_id so the first
  //     live id is 1 and NODE_ID_NONE(0) stays a safe sentinel)
  //   * tail_alloc / tail_free over that pool
  //   * the real parent-liveness half of a11y_node_mint's tree_ref gate
  //     (a11y_tree_row_valid cross-module call into tree_kind.pdx)
  //   * the full ancestor-chain cycle walk NODE_CYCLE's fuller detection
  //     needs beyond a11y_node_no_self_cycle's O(1) trivial guard
  //   * the double-detach guard (an "already detached" failure code,
  //     mirroring IME_SESSION_ALREADY_REVOKED, added to the 24 free
  //     slots left in this file's failure band)
  //   * cap_handler_a11y_node dispatch wiring into cap_table
  //   * the klog_s1_x1 emission of pdx_kind_a11y_node_meta on a real
  //     successful mint
  // ==========================================================================

  // a11y_node_mint(tree_ref, role, initial_state) -> node_id (future)
  //                                                 | NODE_PARENT_MISSING
  //                                                 | NODE_ROLE_INVALID
  //                                                 | NODE_STATE_CONFLICT
  //                                                 | NODE_NOT_INIT
  //
  // Gate order: tree_ref nonzero -> role in range -> state in range ->
  // (no pool) NODE_NOT_INIT. Three callee-save pushes (rbx=tree_ref,
  // r12=role, r13=initial_state) preserve the three arguments across
  // the three nested validator calls; entry rsp%16==8, three 8-byte
  // pushes give rsp%16==0, exactly what SysV requires at each `call`
  // site below. No further pad needed.
  pub let a11y_node_mint : (u64, u64, u64) -> u64 !{} @{} =
    fn (tree_ref: u64) (role: u64) (initial_state: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "Wave0-B14 G10-M1-002 (paideia-os #2303): LINEAR mint stub. Runs the real tree_ref/role/state gates against SECTION D's validators and returns NODE_NOT_INIT once every gate passes, because no row-pool substrate exists yet in this landing (see SECTION E header). A fabricated node_id would defeat the LINEAR invariant this file exists to establish. Three callee-save pushes (rbx=tree_ref, r12=role, r13=initial_state) leave rsp%16==0 (entry 8 + 3*8=24 -> 32, mod16=0) for the three nested validator calls. Labels prefixed a11y_node_mint_.",
      block: {
        push rbx;
        push r12;
        push r13;

        mov  rbx, rdi;                       // tree_ref
        mov  r12, rsi;                       // role
        mov  r13, rdx;                       // initial_state

        mov  rdi, rbx;
        call a11y_node_tree_ref_valid;
        cmp  rax, 0;
        je   a11y_node_mint_no_tree;

        mov  rdi, r12;
        call a11y_node_role_valid;
        cmp  rax, 0;
        je   a11y_node_mint_bad_role;

        mov  rdi, r13;
        call a11y_node_state_valid;
        cmp  rax, 0;
        je   a11y_node_mint_bad_state;

        mov  rax, 0xFFFFE858;                // NODE_NOT_INIT
        jmp  a11y_node_mint_out;

      a11y_node_mint_no_tree:
        mov  rax, 0xFFFFE85C;                // NODE_PARENT_MISSING
        jmp  a11y_node_mint_out;
      a11y_node_mint_bad_role:
        mov  rax, 0xFFFFE85A;                // NODE_ROLE_INVALID
        jmp  a11y_node_mint_out;
      a11y_node_mint_bad_state:
        mov  rax, 0xFFFFE85B;                // NODE_STATE_CONFLICT

      a11y_node_mint_out:
        pop  r13;
        pop  r12;
        pop  rbx;
        ret
      }
    }

  // a11y_node_attach_child(parent_node_id, child_node_id) -> A11Y_NODE_OK (future)
  //                                                         | NODE_NULL
  //                                                         | NODE_CYCLE
  //                                                         | NODE_NOT_INIT
  //
  // Pure leaf: both id checks and the trivial self-cycle guard are
  // inlined directly (equivalent to a11y_node_id_valid x2 +
  // a11y_node_no_self_cycle) rather than calling those functions, so
  // this body needs no stack frame and no alignment bookkeeping at all
  // -- the same "stay a pure leaf, inline the one-instruction check"
  // discipline documented on SECTION D's a11y_node_id_valid /
  // a11y_node_no_self_cycle entries.
  pub let a11y_node_attach_child : (u64, u64) -> u64 !{} @{} =
    fn (parent_node_id: u64) (child_node_id: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "Wave0-B14 G10-M1-002 (paideia-os #2303): ATTACH_CHILD stub. Inlines the id-nonzero and trivial-self-cycle checks directly (pure leaf, no nested call, no stack frame needed) rather than calling a11y_node_id_valid / a11y_node_no_self_cycle. Returns NODE_NOT_INIT once both checks pass, because no row-pool substrate exists yet to actually link the nodes (see SECTION E header) -- a fabricated success would defeat the LINEAR invariant this file establishes. Labels prefixed a11y_node_ac_.",
      block: {
        cmp rdi, 0;
        je  a11y_node_ac_null;

        cmp rsi, 0;
        je  a11y_node_ac_null;

        cmp rdi, rsi;
        je  a11y_node_ac_cycle;

        mov rax, 0xFFFFE858;                 // NODE_NOT_INIT
        ret;

      a11y_node_ac_null:
        mov rax, 0xFFFFE859;                 // NODE_NULL
        ret;

      a11y_node_ac_cycle:
        mov rax, 0xFFFFE85D;                 // NODE_CYCLE
        ret
      }
    }

  // a11y_node_detach(node_id) -> A11Y_NODE_OK (future)
  //                             | NODE_NULL
  //                             | NODE_NOT_INIT
  //
  // Pure leaf, same inline-check rationale as a11y_node_attach_child.
  // This is the LINEAR consume: the future substrate's real body MUST
  // stamp a `revoked`/`in_use=0` state and MUST refuse a second detach
  // against the same node_id (mirroring session_kind.pdx's REVOKE /
  // IME_SESSION_ALREADY_REVOKED contract). This stub does neither --
  // there is no row state yet to stamp or to double-check against.
  pub let a11y_node_detach : (u64) -> u64 !{} @{} =
    fn (node_id: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "Wave0-B14 G10-M1-002 (paideia-os #2303): DETACH stub (the LINEAR consume). Inlines the id-nonzero check directly (pure leaf, no nested call). Returns NODE_NOT_INIT once the check passes, because no row-pool substrate exists yet to actually free the node (see SECTION E header) -- a fabricated success, or a stamped revoke bit with no backing row, would defeat the LINEAR invariant this file establishes. The real double-detach guard (mirroring IME_SESSION_ALREADY_REVOKED) is deferred to the substrate landing, which has live row state to check against. Labels prefixed a11y_node_dt_.",
      block: {
        cmp rdi, 0;
        je  a11y_node_dt_null;

        mov rax, 0xFFFFE858;                 // NODE_NOT_INIT
        ret;

      a11y_node_dt_null:
        mov rax, 0xFFFFE859;                 // NODE_NULL
        ret
      }
    }
}
